# Card

`Card` is the **base representation type** of the knowledge domain: a document
that gives a piece of knowledge a physical form.

```hql
type Card : Doc {
    // the document represents a piece of knowledge
    // the document has a place in a vault
}
```

The narrowing conditions do not include the body's format. A plain Markdown file
gives knowledge a representation as readily as an `.hmd` one — see
[[hmd-integration]]. Card-ness is a knowledge notion.

Everything `Doc` provides — a `Data` header, a body, sections, anchors, links —
a card has, because a card is one. This page is about what a card has *in
addition*, and that addition is the point of the type.

## Two kinds of card

`Card` deliberately says nothing about what the card is *about*. That is
declared by its two subtypes:

```hql
type ConceptCard : {Card, Concept}  // its subject is a concept — a node
type RelationCard : Card             // its subject is a relation — an edge
```

`{Card, Concept}` is the normalized supertype set: `Card : Doc` already holds,
so `{Card, Concept, Doc}` would name `Doc` twice — see [[type-system]]. The
third parent, `Concept`, is what puts a concept card in a node position in a
[[graph-type|knowledge graph]].

Most knowledge lives in nodes, so `ConceptCard` is the ordinary case and is the
default. A `RelationCard` is the exception, opted into explicitly:

```hql
card.metadata.knowledge.type : KnowledgeKind   // Concept | Relation
```

A card that declares nothing is a concept card. `knowledge.type == Relation`
makes it a relation card, and — like every refinement — the entry is an author's
claim that has to be checked against a contract rather than trusted. See
[[hql/knowledge]] and the [knowledge model](../../../models/domain/knowledge.md)
for what each kind does.

## A card is a document that knows where it is

A bare document is a file. A card is a file with a place in a **vault**, and
that place is what makes it addressable by name.

The vault boundary is HMD's. A namespace is a rooted tree of modules, and
resolution never crosses into a neighbouring vault: two vaults are two
namespaces. A card therefore has a name that other cards can write in double
brackets, a spine of ancestor modules that a bare name is probed against, and a
set of documents that may point at it.

This is what turns document machinery into card machinery:

- `[[alice]]` resolves, because there is a namespace to resolve it in.
- `downlinks` are answerable, because the vault knows what points at this card.
- `header.path` means something, because there is a root to be relative to.
- `import` and `use` in the header inherit or not, because there are ancestor
  folder notes to inherit from.

A plain Markdown file dropped into a vault becomes a card by acquiring that
context, not by being rewritten. That is the practical content of HMD's claim
that every `.md` file is already valid `.hmd`.

## A card is a knowledge participant

The difference that matters is not the file format. `card` is a term from
knowledge management, and a card is the kind of document that can reference
knowledge and operate on knowledge concepts.

```hql
knowledge : Knowledge
```

Knowledge is designed from day one, not deferred behind a release boundary. A
card's body, links, header and embeds are not merely content to be rendered:
they are the material that concepts, relations, propositions and evidence are
drawn from. A type system that treats cards as documents first and knowledge as
a later projection gets the dependency backwards, because the reason cards exist
is to carry meaning that a file system cannot.

Concretely, a card can:

- be a **`ConceptCard`** — an identified thing the vault has something to say
  about, and a node in the knowledge graph.
- be a **`RelationCard`** — a relationship promoted into a document so that it
  gains a body, a name in the vault and incoming links. It represents an edge; it
  does not become a node.
- carry **propositions** in its body, which other cards may support or dispute.
- supply **evidence**, as ordinary sections with ordinary links.

None of this is automatic. A link is structural connectivity and does not assert
a semantic relation; a header entry is an author's claim and not a proof of
conformance. The path from document to knowledge runs through explicit
interpretation, and the rule used must be retained with the result. See
[[hql/knowledge]] for the semantic universe and what it retains: conflicting
claims, provenance, constraints and satisfaction.

`Doc` has no such capability and does not need one. A constructed body, an
embedded fragment or an ordinary Markdown file outside any vault is a perfectly
good document with nothing to say about knowledge. The narrowing is exactly the
step at which meaning becomes possible.

## A card's metadata

A card has a second layer the document does not, and it has its own name:

```hql
type Card : Doc {
    metadata : Data     // the knowledge attached to this card
}
```

`header` and `metadata` are not two spellings of one field. A header is a
document-level notion — name, path, format, title, whatever an author wrote and
whatever an extension contributed about the file. Metadata is a knowledge-level
one: concepts, relations, propositions and evidence attached to this card, which
is exactly the addition that makes a card more than a document. Only a card has
it, because only a card participates in knowledge.

That is why `metadata` belongs to [[hql/knowledge]] rather than to [[doc-type]],
and why the knowledge extension refines `card.metadata` while a `git` or
database extension contributes to `card.header`. Both refine one unchanged tree
in the same way — see [[type-system]].

### `card.metadata` is assembled, not inherited

`card.metadata` is **not** the authored `header.metadata` under another name. The
two are different fields with different semantics, and both stay addressable:

```hql
card.header.metadata?   // optional — what the author wrote, if anything
card.metadata           // always present — what the system knows
```

The authored block is optional, because authoring metadata is a choice. The
card's own layer is not, because it exists whether or not the file said anything.
It is assembled when the document becomes a card, from inputs that include:

- the authored `header.metadata`, where the document has one;
- system metadata supplied at load;
- entries contributed by extensions — `git`, an indexer, a link checker;
- vault state held under `.hmd/**`;
- semantic-search state and other derived information.

Authored metadata is one input among these and does not win by being authored.
Which inputs may overwrite which is the **Card metadata precedence**, and it is
this card's to settle; it is not settled yet.

A query has to know which layer it is addressing. `card.header.metadata` answers
*what did the author write?*; `card.metadata` answers *what does the system know
about this card?* A linter checking that authors tagged their notes wants the
first, a search ranking cards wants the second, and confusing them reports the
vault's own bookkeeping as something a person typed. See [[fields]] for the
two-layer distinction in full.

`metadata.knowledge.type` lives in the assembled layer, which is why the choice
between `ConceptCard` and `RelationCard` is a knowledge-level one and not a
filename or folder convention.

What a card's metadata contains before any extension declares its shape is open,
as are the precedence rules above.

## A card's header

A card's header is a [[data-type|Data]] tree like any document's, assembled from
the same derived, authored and contributed layers, and it is readable like any
other field — nothing about a card hides it. Two things are worth stating here,
though neither is what makes a document a card.

First, `header.format` is what licenses wikilinks, embeds and fragment
addressing in the body. A card written in `hmd` has them; a card written in `md`
has a header and a body like any other document, and no hyper layer to ask
about.

Second, where the body is `hmd`, HyperMarkDown reserves exactly four authored
header keys and leaves every other key to the author:

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `tags` | `/`-separated hierarchical strings; categorize, but do not resolve |
| `use` | Per-card feature toggles; inherits from the nearest ancestor folder note |
| `import` | Name bindings and search origins; applies to this card only, never inherits |
| `nav` | `order` and `visibility` for publication |

Projecting these as stronger HQL types is sound, because HMD validates them. The
rest of the tree stays `Data`, and that openness is deliberate: a type system
with a declared header record would have to grow a field every time a vault
invented a key.

```hql
card.header.tags
card.header.nav.visibility
```

What a card whose authored header block fails to parse projects to is
unresolved — a card
with an empty header, a card carrying a diagnostic, or no card at all.

## cards

`cards` is a reserved keyword. It denotes every card in the current namespace
vault:

```hql
cards : Set<Card>
```

This is a context value, not a function call and not a filesystem sweep written
by the user. It has the same horizon a wikilink does: `cards` in one vault never
contains a card from another.

```hql
cards
| typed RelationCard
| graph
```

Being a keyword rather than a binding means a card cannot shadow it by being
named `cards`, and that a program never has to state where the vault is. What
`cards` means inside a nested module — the whole vault, or the module subtree —
still needs a decision, as does whether imported namespaces contribute to it.

There is no matching keyword for documents, and there should not be. `cards` is
bounded because a vault is bounded; documents in general are not a collection
anything can enumerate.

`cards` is a `Set<Card>`: discovering files establishes no ordering guarantee.
A reproducible prefix does not need one, because a card carries its own
ordering key — see [[orderable]] and [[hql/collections]].

## Extensions extend the header

The header is the extension boundary, and an [[hql/extensions|extension]] can
extend it in two quite different senses. Keeping them apart matters.

**Contributing entries.** Some extensions know facts the file does not state — a
`git` extension supplying authorship and revision, an indexer supplying its
embedding and which index the card belongs to, a link checker supplying
resolution diagnostics, a build supplying publication state.

A **score** is not among them. An embedding is a property of the card: it exists
whether or not anyone ever searches, and it goes stale when the card's content
changes, so the assembled metadata layer is its home. A score exists only
relative to a query, and storing one per card would mean either one privileged
query or a number whose name no longer describes it. A score belongs to the
retrieval result, where it travels with the query and the model revision that
produced it — see [[hql/semantic-search]].

```hql
git.metadata(card) : Data
```

This is the second reason the header is broader than what an author writes:
most of what a mature vault knows about a card was never typed into the card.

**Contributing type knowledge.** Other extensions add nothing to the tree at all.
They declare what an existing region of it means, so a subtree that was `Data`
becomes usable at a stronger type without being converted or copied.

```hql
import knowledge

card.metadata          : Data              // before
card.metadata          : KnowledgeMetadata
card.metadata.concepts : List<Concept>   // see below
```

> `List` is no longer part of the decided collection vocabulary, so this
> occurrence needs a semantic choice between `Seq<Concept>` and `Set<Concept>`.
> It is left unresolved deliberately: the answer follows from whether the order
> of a card's concepts carries information, not from the bracket migration. See
> [[hql/collections]].

The runtime value is unchanged; what changed is what the checker can prove about
it. An extension owns type knowledge about a header, not the header itself —
[[type-system]] develops this as open structural data with progressive
refinement, and it is the mechanism behind the narrowing below.

The core's obligation is to keep contributions distinguishable. A header entry
that was derived, authored or contributed is not interchangeable with the
others, and provenance records where a value came from rather than conferring
authority on it. How contributed entries are namespaced, whether an extension
may overwrite an author's key, and what happens when two extensions contribute
the same path are open — though an extension that only contributes type
knowledge raises none of those questions, which is an argument for preferring
that form.

## Types narrow

`Card : Doc` is the first narrowing, not a special case. The same mechanism
continues past it, and it is the same mechanism by which a `Data` subtree
becomes a known shape — see [[type-system]].

```hql
type Person : Card {
    name: String
    born: Date?
}
```

Declaration syntax is illustrative. The direction is not: a `Person` card keeps
its body, its links and its own identity, and other cards can link to it.
Narrowing adds guarantees; it never replaces the card with a record.

`ConceptCard` and `RelationCard` are narrowings of this kind too — the two the
domain declares rather than ones a vault invents.

What is **not** a narrowing is the relation a relation card is about. `Relation`
is a first-class knowledge type, never a specialization of `Card`: it can be
imported, derived by a rule or read out of a store with no document anywhere.
The card represents one, and the relation is reached through the card's
knowledge layer:

```hql
card.metadata.knowledge.type == Relation
card.metadata.relation : Relation<S, T>
```

That is refinement, reached the same way as any other metadata — see
[[type-system]] for why representation and ancestry are different mechanisms,
[[graph-type]] for the edge a relation narrows and [[hql/knowledge]] for why
significant relationships are preferably given cards.

Narrowing has to be checked. A `knowledge.type: Relation` entry in the metadata
is an author's claim, and `typed RelationCard` must verify the contract rather
than trust the label — an unchecked `as` would let any YAML key mint a type. The result of a failed
narrowing, the spelling of validation and refinement, and whether narrowing is
nominal or structural remain open.

```hql
person : Person = validate<Person>(card)
```

Narrowing is also how a vault stays usable while it is being typed. A document
that satisfies nothing beyond `Doc` is still a first-class value; a card that
satisfies nothing beyond `Card` is still in `cards` and still traversable.
Stronger types are earned where there is a reason for them, and the untyped
remainder is not an error.

See [[doc-type]] for the document a card narrows, [[data-type]] for the header's
tree type, [[hql/knowledge]] for the semantic universe, [[pipes]] for composition
and [[hql/design-status]] for what is still undecided.
